 ## Overview
-Levenberg-Marquardt (LM) Pose Optimization is an iterative numerical method that estimates the 6-DoF camera pose by minimizing the squared error between features extracted from a reference image and features rendered from a current pose guess [S1]. It distinguishes itself from gradient-based differentiable rendering approaches by relying on a numerically estimated Jacobian via finite-difference pose perturbations rather than analytical gradients [S1].
+Levenberg-Marquardt Pose Optimization is an iterative procedure that minimizes the squared error between reference image features and rendered image features by using a numerically estimated Jacobian [S1]. It sets itself apart from direct analytical gradient methods by relying on finite-difference approximations of the Jacobian via pose perturbations, which bypasses the complex analytical description of the image formation model involving lens and illumination dependencies [S1]. It builds on [Bundle Adjustment](8540-bundle-adjustment.md) by focusing specifically on the pose update step within the optimization loop.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Reference image $s_{ref}$, initial pose guess $\hat{p}_0$, Levenberg-Marquardt damping parameter $\lambda$, convergence threshold $\epsilon$, number of pose samples $N_s$ [S1].
-**Outputs:** Converged pose $\hat{p}_{final}$ [S1].
+**Inputs:** Reference image $s_{ref}$, initial pose guess $\hat{p}_0$, Levenberg-Marquardt damping parameter $\lambda$, convergence threshold $\epsilon$.
+**Outputs:** Converged pose $\hat{p}_{final}$.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   Pose vector $\hat{p}$: 6-dimensional vector representing rotation and translation [S1].
-*   Feature vector $\tilde{x}$: Extracted features from $s_{ref}$ [S1].
-*   Jacobian $J_f$: Numerically estimated Jacobian of feature errors with respect to pose parameters [S1].
+*   Pose vector $\hat{p}$: Represents the 6-DoF camera pose (rotation and translation) in the $SE(3)$ group.
+*   Feature vector $\tilde{x}$: Extracted features from the reference image.
+*   Jacobian $J_f$: Numerically estimated Jacobian matrix mapping pose perturbations to feature errors.
 
 **Steps:**
-1.  **Initialization:** Extract reference features $\tilde{x}$ from $s_{ref}$ [S1].
-2.  **Iteration:** While the norm of the pose update $\|\delta p\| > \epsilon$:
-    a.  **Render and Match:** Render the scene at the current pose guess $\hat{p}_i$ [S1]. Extract features $h(s_i, \hat{p}_i)$ and match them with $\tilde{x}$ [S1].
-    b.  **Pose Sampling:** Sample $N_s$ pose parameters around the current guess $\hat{p}_i$ [S1]. The rotation axis for perturbations is sampled uniformly on a spherical surface, biasing samples to a $\delta$-neighborhood of the reference pose [S1].
-    c.  **Jacobian Estimation:** Render images at the sampled pose values [S1]. Match reference features in each rendered image [S1]. Calculate the Jacobian $J_f$ using the perturbation method (Eq. 13 in [S1]), which approximates the derivative of the feature error with respect to the pose [S1].
-    d.  **Update:** Calculate the least-squares pose update $\hat{p}_{i+1}$ using the Levenberg-Marquardt update rule (Eq. 16 in [S1]) [S1]. The update $\delta p$ is applied to $\hat{p}_i$ to get $\hat{p}_{i+1}$ [S1].
-3.  **Termination:** Return $\hat{p}_{final}$ when $\|\delta p\| \le \epsilon$ [S1].
