 ### [Derivative-free Pose Estimation via Feature Matching](11310-derivative-free-pose-estimation-via-feature-matching.md)
-This method estimates the 6-DoF pose of a target from a single reference image by rendering multiple images at sampled pose perturbations, extracting feature correspondences, and computing a least-squares estimate for the pose using differential corrections to an initial guess. Unlike its sibling [Differentiable Rendering for Pose Estimation](11554-differentiable-rendering-for-pose-estimation.md), which explicitly constructs a numerical Jacobian of the image with respect to pose perturbations to drive gradient-based optimization, this method relies on differential corrections to an initial guess via feature matching. It bypasses the analytical description of the image formation model, which is complex due to lens and illumination dependencies, by seeking a local numerical solution in the feature space.
+Estimates 6-DoF pose by rendering images at sampled perturbations, extracting features, and using differential corrections. It bypasses analytical image formation models by seeking a local numerical solution in feature space, unlike methods that explicitly construct a numerical Jacobian.
 
-### Lie Algebra (Tangent Plane) Pose Optimization
-A gradient-based optimization approach that solves for pose estimation variables $\xi$ in the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{se}(3)$ (tangent plane) rather than directly on the $SE(3)$ manifold, providing smoother and quicker convergence [S2]. The loss function is formulated as $L = \sum_{u \in R} \|I(F(T K u; \theta)) - I(u)\|^2$, where $T$ is the transformation from camera to world coordinates, $K$ are camera intrinsic parameters, and $R$ is a sampled set of pixel coordinates [S2]. The optimal pose is recovered via $T = \exp(\hat{\xi}) T_0$ [S2]. Optimization on the tangent plane is superior to direct optimization on $SE(3)$, which leads to incorrect estimation results due to noisy photometric loss landscapes [S2].
+### [Lie Algebra (Tangent Plane) Pose Optimization](14005-lie-algebra-tangent-plane-pose-optimization.md)
+Optimizes in the tangent space of $SE(3)$ ($\mathfrak{se}(3)$) while keeping voxel embeddings fixed. It achieves smoother convergence than direct optimization on the $SE(3)$ manifold, which is prone to errors from noisy photometric loss landscapes.
 
